{"chunk_id": "4a25ccff90b6", "text": "Krishna contrasts two fundamental human dispositions: the divine (god\u2011like) and the demoniac. Divine qualities include fearlessness, purity, self\u2011discipline, truthfulness, compassion, humility, and freedom from anger or greed\u2014traits that lead toward liberation. Demoniac traits such as hypocrisy, pride, arrogance, anger, cruelty, and ignorance bind beings to suffering and destructive behavior. Those dominated by ego and insatiable desire view the world as purposeless and pursue wealth, power, and pleasure while trapped in delusion. Krishna warns that lust, wrath, and avarice are the three gates to hell and must be renounced. The section ends by asserting that scripture should guide moral action. In the opening of Chapter XVII, Krishna explains that faith and practice vary according to the three qualities (goodness, passion, darkness), influencing worship, austerity, and even food preferences.\nKrishna contrasts divine and demonic qualities, warns against lust, anger, and greed, and explains how faith, food, sacrifice, and discipline reflect the three gunas.\nThemes: divine vs demonic nature, three gunas, ethical conduct, self-discipline, authority of scripture", "vector": [0.01661856099963188, 0.00986192561686039, 0.0019440953619778156, -0.012776423245668411, -0.013342217542231083, 0.014842230826616287, -0.035131875425577164, -0.00844086054712534, -0.02960551343858242, -0.026092324405908585, 0.00640136981382966, 0.005789522547274828, -0.006993480026721954, 0.008164542727172375, -0.014026434160768986, 0.020408065989613533, 0.052553076297044754, -0.028737084940075874, 0.014026434160768986, -0.011026408523321152, -0.016158031299710274, 0.005026358179748058, -0.020184380933642387, -0.01982911489903927, -0.02365809492766857, 0.011296147480607033, 0.007217166014015675, -0.032079216092824936, -0.006733609363436699, -0.011348780244588852, -0.012269840575754642, -0.008164542727172375, 0.0052171493880450726, 0.00246383645571